A||||9||Oreif||Need answers to an Idea. (F.I. vs. Carb)||||1||
Z||000000||Oreif||05-01-2001||06:48 PM||||OK, I am curious, What are the advantages/dis-advantages of going to a 4-BBL carb onto a stock 2.8L. Basically dumping the fuel injection. <BR>I saw Taijiguy's post with the shaker scoop and it got me thinking. Would I lose or gain HP/torque? Would any other work be required to install one other than the manifold and carb? How would I get around the computer?<BR>Any help or idea's let me know, <BR>Thanks,<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wannabe||05-01-2001||07:01 PM||||The only way you will ealize a benefit through this swap is if the engine is modified for high power output at high rpm. Replacing the FIwith a carb by itself will not provide a benefit. The practical limit for the factory intake plenum/manifold is probably in the 200-220 hp range with significant porting. Above this, the runners are too long and too small to allow air flow at the higher rpm required to make power. Once you reach this point, you may see a benefit by going to a carb setup, assuming the new intake is also suited to high rpm service and you can add more cam. The key is getting the right combination of parts - camshaft, intake, heads and exhaust - there is no cheap magic bullet for getting horsepower (nitrous is as close as it gets)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||05-01-2001||07:17 PM||||I am a firm believer in fuel injection.You get more power,better economy.Even if you were building a race engine,if you use injection you can more closely control your fuel mixture to get the most out of your engine.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tryxalon||05-01-2001||07:38 PM||||As one of those guys who used to hop up cars in the 60's/70's with Carbs .... the answer is "yeah, you could go carb ... but you lose the bene's of FI" <P>FI is more precise, better match of Fuel/air mix over larger range. But then this is achieved by computer control. This means enough sensors to feed imput to the computer and ECM that can control the fuel/air, etc. <P>Carb is less precise, but also needs less electronic control, and is simpler.<P>That being said: the ease of achieving the proper power/fuel mixture with greatest ease that people talk about on FI is because the computer does the work.  <P>Carbs can be tuned (but there are fewer that have the skill or patience to do so).  <P>In the late 60's I had a 64 Dodge Polara with a bored 383 and a mild cam. Intake was a customized Holley on a Torquer manifold with very small primaries and toilet bowl secondary jets (manual lockout on the vacume secondaries). <P>  I got 18 to 20 MPG tooling or cruizing but had to drive very gingerly to avoid lean conditions (technically undercarbed engine - a vacume gage on the dash helped). <P>Then when I pulled the cable to unlock the secondaries and "pour it to it" I could run the 4000lb car down the quarter in 14.4. <P>Never had the death wish to find top end. Also couldn't find a road long enough ... fastest was the 140 on the speedometer. <P>Very much an abused engine undercarbed or overrevved, no in-between.  No, you can't pull such tricks with  Fuel Injection - but you shouldn't be able to even with a carb. <P>Using a Carb is possible ... but to get it right takes time and experimentation. In the long run, the FI is the better choice but takes more money. <P>    <p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 05-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dave Gunsul||05-01-2001||08:15 PM||||While I agree with the benifits of FI above<BR>I think a lot of you are forgetting that the<BR>Fieros FI is a speed density controlled FI.<BR>This limits the amount of mods you can do without getting a custom chip. A carb just<BR>has to be tuned right. <BR>Edelbrock makes a performance manifold for a<BR>4-barrel carb, all you would need then is a<BR>non computer ignition system from an earlier<BR>2.8 and you'd be in business. Then you can run a very large cam and high compression.<BR>The thing you've got to ask yourself is: is this really going to get me more than a turbo FI Fiero and be worth the trouble?<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-01-2001||08:58 PM||||I posted a response to your questions in my thread. Whatever you want to know about the swap, I'm more than glad to lend a hand.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||LottaBallsCamaro||05-01-2001||11:27 PM||||I would reccomend keeping the fuel injection, like everyoneabove said, its moe precise, provides more power but it also has better throtle responce, better gas mileage, better emmisions(Keep it green!) and is cooler, however, a carb setup is lighter, more tuner friendly and much cheaper to hop up.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||05-02-2001||11:22 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The practical limit for the factory intake plenum/manifold is probably in the 200-220 hp range with significant porting<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Where did you get that info. from?  I know quite a few people in the 300 Hp range with the Fiero intake setup (ported). I think they would beg to differ....||||reg||5
Z||000008||88formula||05-02-2001||05:04 PM||||If you compared fuel injection to a carburetor and they had the same kind of intake manifolds the only major difference would be that the fuel injected engine would have better gas mileage, a little better derivability, lower emissions, and in some but not all cases a little more horsepower. They are very close to each other in which one has the ability to make more power.  <P>The fuel injection system on the fiero is tuned port fuel injection. This means that the intake runners are very long tuned and narrow intake runners that give the engine very good low rpm torque but sacrifice top end horsepower. There is no difference in power between sequential fuel injection (each injector fires separately at low rpm) compared to batch fire injection (each bank fires alternately from one side to the other) at wide open throttle. Sequential fuel injection has a mileage advantage over batch fire though. Speed density is the type of program that the computer used to calculate the air fuel ratio. A speed density system is only more difficult to modify because any changes in the engines ability to breathe are not compensated for like a mass air flow system unless you change the ECM prom chip with a new calibrated one. The computer has no way to measure the air being pumped by the engine with a speed density system. <P>The advantage of switching over to a carburetor for your 2.8-liter engine is that you can use a different kind of intake manifold that favors horsepower more than torque, which the fieros intake system does. You could add a dual plane intake manifold that when compared to tuned port injection has shorter and bigger intake runners. With this setup you can make more horsepower than a fuel injected fiero with the same engine. The only compromise is that you will loose some bottom end torque and possible get worse gas mileage and have higher emissions.  These are the facts. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||05-02-2001||09:33 PM||||If you are trying to get more top rpm power, here is a combination that I saw on an S-10 a while back:<BR>A dual plane aluminum intake for a 4bbl with the center divider machined out of it, basically making a single plane out of it. It was topped off with a Holley projection throttle body fuel injection. The cam was a little lumpy, headers and dual flowmasters. That thing really hauled! It was still pulling hard at 6,000 rpms and had decent low/mid torque.||||reg||1
